@node D-Bus Events
@section Events Endpoint

@cindex events, D-Bus
@cindex org.cmacs.Editor1.Events

The events endpoint is the one place external tools subscribe to in
order to @emph{observe} a running cmacs: files opened / saved /
closed, buffers created / killed / switched / modified, project
switches, window and frame activity, the editor lifecycle,
fine-grained text edits, and selected cmacs subsystems.

@example
Interface:    org.cmacs.Editor1.Events
Object path:  /org/cmacs/Editor
C source:     cmacs/dbus/cmacs-dbus-iface-events.c
Lisp source:  lisp/cmacs/cmacs-dbus-events.el
Enabled:      by default (core categories), when the service runs
@end example

Emission is driven from Emacs hooks in @file{cmacs-dbus-events.el};
the C side owns the introspection contract and three query methods.
Every emit is fire-and-forget and a @emph{no-op when the service is
down}, so the hooks stay cheap and can never interrupt editing.

@subsection Two ways to consume

Every event is broadcast twice, so you can choose how to subscribe.

@subsubheading The firehose: Event

One signal carries @emph{every} event.  Subscribe to it and you see
everything.

@example
Signal: Event
Body:   s category, s name, s detail, a@{sv@} data, t timestamp_us
@end example

@itemize @bullet
@item
@code{category} --- the family: @code{file}, @code{buffer},
@code{project}, @code{window}, @code{frame}, @code{editor},
@code{text}, @code{gsurf}, @code{ai}, @code{cintrospect}.
@item
@code{name} --- the specific event: @code{opened}, @code{saved},
@code{closed}, @code{created}, @code{killed}, @code{switched},
@code{modified}, @code{unmodified}, @code{startup}, @code{focused}, ...
@item
@code{detail} --- the primary subject (a filename or buffer name) as a
plain string, for cheap @code{arg0}/@code{arg2} match-rule filtering.
@item
@code{data} --- an extensible @code{a@{sv@}} dictionary of structured
fields (@code{:file}, @code{:buffer}, @code{:point}, @code{:root}, ...).
@emph{New keys may be added over time}; ignore unknown keys.
@item
@code{timestamp_us} --- microseconds since the epoch, stamped
server-side.
@end itemize

The firehose is emitted by the @code{cmacs-dbus-emit-event} DEFUN,
which is the only emitter that builds the @code{a@{sv@}} payload (the
generic @code{cmacs-dbus-emit-signal} marshals a plist as @code{av},
not @code{a@{sv@}}).

@subsubheading Named typed signals

Each event also fires a distinct named signal, so a subscriber can ask
the bus to deliver only the kinds it cares about (match on
@code{member}).  Named signals are emitted on the same path /
interface as @code{Event}.

@example
file       FileOpened              s path
file       FileSaved               s path
file       FileClosed              s path
buffer     BufferCreated           s name
buffer     BufferKilled            s name
buffer     BufferSwitched          s name, s previous
buffer     BufferSaved             s name, s file
buffer     BufferModified          s name
buffer     BufferUnmodified        s name
project    ProjectSwitched         s root, s previous
window     WindowSelectionChanged  s buffer, s frame
frame      FrameOpened             s frame
frame      FrameClosed             s frame
frame      FrameFocused            s frame
frame      FrameUnfocused          s frame
editor     EditorStartup           (none)
editor     EditorShutdown          (none)
text       TextChanged             s name, x beg, x end, x length
gsurf      BrowserLoadChanged      s buffer, s state
gsurf      BrowserUriChanged       s buffer, s uri
gsurf      BrowserTitleChanged     s buffer, s title
gsurf      BrowserCrashed          s buffer
ai         AiMessage               s room, s sender, s text
cintrospect PatchApplied           s function
@end example

Modified state is split into two string-only signals
(@code{BufferModified} / @code{BufferUnmodified}) rather than one
boolean signal: Emacs has no boolean false (@code{nil} marshals as
@code{""}), so a @code{b} argument could never carry the unmodified
case correctly.  The same reasoning applies to frame focus.

@subsection Methods

@example
EventTypes ()       -> as   The taxonomy as category/name strings
Categories ()       -> as   The currently-enabled categories
Recent (i count)    -> s    JSON array of the last count events
@end example

@code{Recent} reads a Lisp ring buffer (size
@code{cmacs-dbus-events-ring-size}, default 256), so a tool that
connects @emph{after} something happened can still fetch the recent
backlog --- mirroring @code{Log.RecentMessages}.

@subsection Enabling and configuring

The endpoint is controlled by the global minor mode
@code{cmacs-dbus-events-mode}, which is @emph{on by default}
(auto-enabled from @code{emacs-startup-hook}).  It is a no-op whenever
the service is not running.

Which families emit is controlled by
@code{cmacs-dbus-events-categories}:

@example
;; Default --- core editor activity:
(setq cmacs-dbus-events-categories
      '(file buffer project window frame editor))

;; Opt in to the high-volume text feed and subsystem bridges:
(setq cmacs-dbus-events-categories
      '(file buffer project window frame editor
        text gsurf ai cintrospect))
@end example

Changing this while the mode is on re-installs the hooks immediately.
The high-volume @code{text} category and the subsystem bridges
(@code{gsurf}, @code{ai}, @code{cintrospect}) are off by default.

@example
cmacs-dbus-events-ring-size      256    Events kept for Recent
cmacs-dbus-events-buffer-filter  (fn)   Predicate; skip dull buffers
cmacs-dbus-events-debug          nil    Log emit failures
@end example

@subsubheading Robustness

@itemize @bullet
@item
Every handler is error-guarded --- a misbehaving bridge cannot break
editing; subsystem bridges are also @code{boundp}/feature guarded.
@item
High-frequency sources (@code{buffer-list-update-hook},
@code{after-change-functions}) are debounced via idle timers and
coalesced; the @code{text} category is off by default.
@item
Emission does no Lisp work and never writes @code{*Messages*}, so it
cannot recurse or touch the GLib-loop @code{waiting_for_input} guard.
@end itemize

@subsection Related: per-manager signals

The buffer create / kill handlers also fire the standard
@code{BufferManager.BufferAdded} / @code{BufferRemoved} signals at the
root path @code{/org/cmacs/Editor} (string payloads), which were
previously declared but never emitted.  This lights up
@code{cmacsctl watch stream}.  Frame add / close is exposed through the
Events interface (@code{FrameOpened} / @code{FrameClosed}) rather than
@code{FrameManager.Frame*} (whose @code{i} int32 signature the generic
Lisp emitter cannot produce).

@subsection Subscribing

@subsubheading From the shell

@example
# The firehose, live:
gdbus monitor --session --dest org.cmacs.Editor \
    --object-path /org/cmacs/Editor

# The taxonomy and a recent backlog:
gdbus call --session --dest org.cmacs.Editor \
    --object-path /org/cmacs/Editor \
    --method org.cmacs.Editor1.Events.EventTypes
gdbus call --session --dest org.cmacs.Editor \
    --object-path /org/cmacs/Editor \
    --method org.cmacs.Editor1.Events.Recent 20
@end example

@subsubheading Via emacsctl

@example
cmacsctl events            # stream the firehose
cmacsctl events recent 20  # last 20 events as JSON
cmacsctl events types      # the category/name taxonomy
@end example

@subsubheading From Python (Gio low-level)

@example
from gi.repository import Gio, GLib

def on_event(conn, sender, path, iface, signal, params):
    category, name, detail, data, ts = params.unpack()
    print(f"@{category@}/@{name@} @{detail@} @{dict(data)@}")

bus = Gio.bus_get_sync(Gio.BusType.SESSION)
bus.signal_subscribe(
    "org.cmacs.Editor",                 # sender
    "org.cmacs.Editor1.Events",         # interface
    "Event",                            # the firehose
    "/org/cmacs/Editor",         # path
    None, Gio.DBusSignalFlags.NONE, on_event)
GLib.MainLoop().run()
@end example
